# Isle Connect: where every file goes

There are only **three places** files go: your **website host**, the **Supabase SQL Editor**, and (optional) a **private GitHub repository**.

## 1. Website host (where your site lives)
Upload these to the **same folder** as the site (the "root"):

| File | Notes |
|---|---|
| `index.html` | Your whole site. Before uploading, paste your real Supabase URL and anon key into the `CONFIG` block near the bottom. |
| `robots.txt` (from `security/`) | Must be in the root. Asks AI crawlers to stay out. |
| `_headers` (from `security/`) | Netlify or Cloudflare Pages only. Adds the real security headers. |
| `.htaccess` (from `security/`) | Apache hosting only. Same job as `_headers`. Use one or the other. |

Hosts that can't set headers (for example GitHub Pages): skip `_headers`/`.htaccess` and put the site behind Cloudflare (see `security/SECURITY-SETUP.md`).

## 2. Supabase SQL Editor (Dashboard > SQL Editor > New query > paste > Run)
These do **not** go on the website. Paste each file's contents into the SQL Editor and click Run, **in this order**:

| # | File | What it adds |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | *your original setup file* | Tables, countries, admin, photo/audio storage |
| 2 | `supabase_featured.sql` | "Sponsored" boosts |
| 3 | `supabase_scraper.sql` | Source link + expiry for imported posts (needed even if you never use the weekly import) |
| 4 | `supabase_subscribers_traffic.sql` | Email sign-ups and the Traffic tab |
| 5 | `supabase_security_fix.sql` | Fixes the Supabase advisor warnings |
| 6 | `supabase_questions.sql` | "Ask a question" chat inbox |
| 7 | `supabase_post_ratings.sql` | Visitor thumbs up/down on posts |
| 8 | `supabase_post_videos.sql` | Video uploads |
| 9 | `supabase_broadcast.sql` | Admin "Post to all": publish one post to many countries at once |
| 10 | `isle_connect_posts_by_country.sql` | Optional: the jobs and holidays we collected earlier |
| 11 | `isle_connect_starter_content.sql` | Optional but recommended: 2027 holiday calendars for all 28 countries, 22 festival posts, and a hurricane-readiness post for every country |
| 12 | `isle_connect_emergency_numbers.sql` | Optional but recommended: emergency numbers for all 28 countries |
| 13 | `supabase_security_hardening.sql` | **Always last.** Hides contact details from the public list, adds rate limits. Re-run it any time you add a column to `posts` |

**Important:** run step 13 and upload the new `index.html` **at the same time**. The new page knows how to fetch contacts one by one; an older `index.html` would show "couldn't load posts" once step 10 has run.

## 3. Private GitHub repository (optional, only for the weekly job/scholarship import)
Upload the `scraper/` folder and `.github/workflows/weekly-scrape.yml`, then add the four secrets listed in `scraper/README.md`. Nothing from this step goes on your website or into Supabase.

## 4. Files that just stay with you
`security/SECURITY-SETUP.md` (read it), `isle_connect_monetization.xlsx` (your planning workbook).

## After uploading: quick check
1. Open the site in a private window and hard-refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R).
2. Open an island: posts load, "Show contact details" works, thumbs up/down work.
3. Sign in at your private admin address and look at the Posts, Ratings, Questions, Subscribers and Traffic tabs.
4. Press F12 > Console: there should be no red "blocked by Content Security Policy" messages. If there are, tell me what they say.

## Before you launch: checklist
1. **Fill in your details.** Open `index.html`, find `SITE_OPERATOR` and `CONTACT_EMAIL` near the top of the script, and replace the placeholders. They appear on the Privacy and Terms pages. Signed in as admin, those pages show a reminder until you do. The pages are plain-language templates written from what the site does, not legal advice: have someone who knows the law where you operate read them.
2. **Check your own emergency numbers and holidays.** They were compiled from public lists, checked against several sources where I could. Confirm them with your government's website, and edit any post in the admin screen.
3. **Check the weekly import.** Run the GitHub workflow once with "dry run" ticked and look at what it would add before running it for real.
4. **Look for blocked requests.** Press F12 > Console on the live site: there should be no red "Content Security Policy" messages.
5. **Add real photos and a few local posts** on your first 3 to 5 islands before you promote the site. A page with 10 to 15 posts looks alive; an empty one looks abandoned.
